Subject: [TEST builds only] PCI: qcom: keep the power domain on while the link is kept in system suspend

Test builds only.  scripts/build-mainline-kernel.sh applies this only with
APPLY_SM8550_PCIE0_GDSC_GUARD=1 (which goes together with
APPLY_SM8550_PCIE0_GDSC_OFF=1), and refuses a build without the variable in
a source tree that carries it.  No board check.  It is the PCIe host's half
of the guard; the clock controller's is the genpd flag that
gcc-sm8550-pcie0-gdsc-off-in-suspend.patch gives pcie_0_gdsc.

1. The guard.  qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq() knows whether the host kept its
   link: dw_pcie_suspend_noirq() keeps it, and leaves pci->suspended false,
   when a bound function on the bus, the root port included, is not in
   D3hot (or has wakeup on and no PME from D3cold:
   pci_host_common_d3cold_possible()), and powers the host down otherwise,
   or when the link is down.  It now tells genpd: device_set_awake_path()
   when the link is kept, device_set_out_band_wakeup() when the host powered
   down, each only for a suspend_noirq that succeeded.
   genpd's genpd_finish_suspend() runs this callback first and then, for a
   domain with GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP, leaves the domain on for a device
   on the awake path that is not out-of-band, and genpd_finish_resume() reads
   the same two flags before it would power the domain on (they are cleared
   only in the next suspend's device_prepare()), so the two stay balanced.
   The awake path set this late, in the noirq phase, reaches no parent:
   dpm_propagate_wakeup_to_parent() runs in the suspend and late suspend
   phases only.  out-of-band matters only when a wakeup-enabled function
   below put the host on the awake path in those phases: genpd then powers
   the domain off for a host that is down all the same.  Nothing else in the
   tree reads either flag of this device.  A domain without the genpd flag
   ignores both: pcie_1_gdsc, and every other qcom PCIe host's in this
   tree's device trees (each host's power domain is a GDSC of its clock
   controller, and no other GDSC has the flag), so nothing changes there,
   and a board with two hosts keeps the second's behaviour.

2. A record, for test builds: a genpd power notifier on the host's domain
   (dev_pm_genpd_add_notifier(), at the end of a probe that succeeded) notes
   a power-off between suspend_noirq and resume_noirq, and the resume logs
   one line per system sleep, which tools/inplace/typec-roles/verify.sh and
   sleep-test.sh read:
     qcom-pcie 1c00000.pcie: system sleep: the link was kept, and its power domain stayed on
     qcom-pcie 1c00000.pcie: system sleep: the host powered down, and genpd powered its power domain off
   and the two that should not happen with the guard, the first a warning:
     ... the link was kept, but genpd powered its power domain off
     ... the host powered down, and its power domain stayed on
   With pcie_0_gdsc in retention (PWRSTS_RET_ON, sleep-test.sh
   gdsc-ret=pcie0) genpd's power-off leaves it on in hardware: "genpd
   powered its power domain off" is what genpd did.  A pm_test cycle prints
   its line as well (the boot's panel cycle among them).  Info lines,
   so below a console loglevel of 7 they go to the log only.

Why.  pcie_0_gdsc PWRSTS_OFF_ON (gcc-sm8550-pcie0-gdsc-off-in-suspend.patch)
is what the SM-X710's sleeps with the PCIe host powered down needed (kit
revisions 9 and 10: 7 of 7 with it collapsed, 0 of 8 without), and it
collapsed the domain under a host that kept its link as well: every
suspend with the Wi-Fi bound, the boot's panel cycle among them.  967e011013ed
upstream keeps the PCIe GDSCs in retention for that reason.  With the guard
the domain collapses only when the host has powered itself down.

--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <linux/pci.h>
 #include <linux/pci-ecam.h>
 #include <linux/pci-pwrctrl.h>
+#include <linux/pm_domain.h>
 #include <linux/pm_opp.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
@@ -292,6 +293,15 @@
 	struct list_head ports;
 	struct gpio_desc *reset;
 	bool use_pm_opp;
+	/*
+	 * System sleep: whether the last system suspend kept the link
+	 * (qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq()), and, recorded by pd_nb for the line the
+	 * resume logs, whether genpd powered the host's power domain off since.
+	 */
+	bool link_kept;
+	bool pd_off;
+	bool pd_nb_registered;
+	struct notifier_block pd_nb;
 };
 
 #define to_qcom_pcie(x)		dev_get_drvdata((x)->dev)
@@ -1980,6 +1990,65 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A host that keeps its link through a system suspend needs its power domain
+ * on, or the controller loses its state under the live link and its resume
+ * breaks (why SM8550's PCIe GDSCs are PWRSTS_RET_ON upstream, 967e011013ed).
+ * genpd leaves the domain on for a device on the awake path when the domain
+ * has GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP, and powers it on again for the resume the
+ * same way (genpd_finish_suspend() and genpd_finish_resume(),
+ * drivers/pmdomain/core.c).  A host that powered itself down needs the
+ * domain no more, whatever its endpoints' wakeup settings are (an endpoint
+ * that wakes the system with the host powered down does so out of band, by
+ * WAKE#): out_band_wakeup lets genpd power the domain off even when a
+ * wakeup-enabled function below set the awake path in the suspend phase
+ * (dpm_propagate_wakeup_to_parent(), drivers/base/power/main.c).  Both last
+ * until the next suspend's prepare.  A domain without the flag ignores both.
+ * Test builds only (pcie-qcom-keep-power-domain-while-link-kept.patch, with
+ * gcc-sm8550-pcie0-gdsc-off-in-suspend.patch for SM8550's pcie_0_gdsc).
+ */
+static void qcom_pcie_mark_sleep_path(struct device *dev, bool link_kept)
+{
+	if (link_kept)
+		device_set_awake_path(dev);
+	else
+		device_set_out_band_wakeup(dev);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Test builds: what genpd did with the host's power domain in a system sleep,
+ * for the line qcom_pcie_report_sleep() logs at the resume.  The power-off,
+ * when genpd makes one, comes after qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq() returns, and
+ * the power-on before qcom_pcie_resume_noirq() is called.
+ */
+static int qcom_pcie_pd_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action, void *data)
+{
+	struct qcom_pcie *pcie = container_of(nb, struct qcom_pcie, pd_nb);
+
+	if (action == GENPD_NOTIFY_OFF)
+		WRITE_ONCE(pcie->pd_off, true);
+
+	return NOTIFY_OK;
+}
+
+static void qcom_pcie_report_sleep(struct qcom_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	struct device *dev = pcie->pci->dev;
+	bool off = READ_ONCE(pcie->pd_off);
+
+	if (!pcie->pd_nb_registered)
+		return;
+
+	if (pcie->link_kept && off)
+		dev_warn(dev, "system sleep: the link was kept, but genpd powered its power domain off\n");
+	else if (pcie->link_kept)
+		dev_info(dev, "system sleep: the link was kept, and its power domain stayed on\n");
+	else if (off)
+		dev_info(dev, "system sleep: the host powered down, and genpd powered its power domain off\n");
+	else
+		dev_info(dev, "system sleep: the host powered down, and its power domain stayed on\n");
+}
+
 static int qcom_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct qcom_pcie_perst *perst, *tmp_perst;
@@ -2140,6 +2209,10 @@
 	if (pcie->mhi)
 		qcom_pcie_init_debugfs(pcie);
 
+	/* Test builds: see qcom_pcie_report_sleep(). */
+	pcie->pd_nb.notifier_call = qcom_pcie_pd_notify;
+	pcie->pd_nb_registered = !dev_pm_genpd_add_notifier(dev, &pcie->pd_nb);
+
 	return 0;
 
 err_phy_exit:
@@ -2165,6 +2238,8 @@
 	if (!pcie)
 		return 0;
 
+	WRITE_ONCE(pcie->pd_off, false);
+
 	ret = dw_pcie_suspend_noirq(pcie->pci);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
@@ -2211,6 +2286,12 @@
 				dev_pm_opp_set_opp(pcie->pci->dev, NULL);
 		}
 	}
+
+	if (!ret) {
+		pcie->link_kept = !pcie->pci->suspended;
+		qcom_pcie_mark_sleep_path(dev, pcie->link_kept);
+	}
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -2223,6 +2304,8 @@
 	if (!pcie)
 		return 0;
 
+	qcom_pcie_report_sleep(pcie);
+
 	if (pcie->pci->suspended) {
 		if (pcie->use_pm_opp) {
 			ret = qcom_pcie_set_max_opp(dev);
